This section lists the LMFDB definitions in the subcategory `mf.hilbert`. Each definition links back to its LMFDB knowl.

:::definition "mf.hilbert" (lean := "HilbertModularForm, HilbertModularForm.IsCuspForm, HilbertModularForm.automorphyFactor, HilbertModularForm.VanishesAtCusps, HilbertModularGroup.gammaZero")
*Hilbert modular form.* ([LMFDB](https://beta.lmfdb.org/knowledge/show/mf.hilbert))

Let $`F` be a {bpref "nf.totally_real"}[totally real] {bpref "nf"}[number field] $`F` of {bpref "nf.degree"}[degree] $`n>1`.  Let $`v_1,\ldots,v_n \colon F \hookrightarrow \R` be the real embeddings of $`F`, and for $`a \in F` abbreviate $`a_i \colonequals v_i(a)` and extend this elementwise to matrices.  Write $`\alpha \in F_{>0}^\times` if $`\alpha` is {bpref "nf.totally_positive"}[totally positive] and $`\Z_{F,>0}^\times \colonequals \Z_F^\times \cap F_{>0}^\times`.

Let $`k_1,\dots,k_n \geq 2` be positive integers of the same parity, and let $`\mathfrak{N}` be a nonzero ideal of the {bpref "nf.ring_of_integers"}[ring of integers] $`\Z_F` of $`F`.

For an ideal $`\mathfrak{b} \subseteq \Z_F`, let

$$`\gamma\in\Gamma_0(\mathfrak{N})_{\mathfrak{b}} \colonequals \left\{\gamma=\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} \in \GL_2(F) : c \in \mathfrak{Nb}, \ b \in \mathfrak{b}^{-1},\ \text{ and } \det(\gamma) \in \Z_{F,>0}^\times \right\}.`

A *Hilbert modular form* of weight $`(k_1\ldots,k_n)` and level $`\mathfrak{N}` is a tuple $`(f_{\mathfrak{b}})_{\mathfrak{b}}` of holomorphic functions $`f \colon \mathcal{H}^n\rightarrow \C`, indexed by ideals $`\mathfrak{b}` representing the {bpref "nf.narrow_class_group"}[narrow class group] of $`\Z_F`, such that for all $`z=(z_1\ldots,z_n)\in\mathcal{H}^n` and all $`\gamma=\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} \in \Gamma_0(\mathfrak{N})_{\mathfrak{b}}` we have

$$`f_{\mathfrak{b}}(\gamma z)=f_{\mathfrak{b}}\left(\frac{a_1z_1+b_1}{c_1z_1+d_1}, \ldots, \frac{a_nz_n+b_n}{c_nz_n+d_n}\right)=\prod_{i=1}^n\left( \frac{(c_iz_i+d_i)^{k_i}}{(a_id_i-b_ic_i)^{k_i/2}}\right)f_{\mathfrak{b}}(z).`

A *Hilbert cusp form* is a Hilbert modular form that vanishes at the cusps.

Depends on: {uses "nf"}[] {uses "nf.degree"}[] {uses "nf.narrow_class_group"}[] {uses "nf.ring_of_integers"}[] {uses "nf.totally_positive"}[] {uses "nf.totally_real"}[]
:::

:::definition "mf.hilbert.dimension" (lean := "HilbertModularForm.heckeOrbitDimension, HilbertModularForm.rationalHeckeOrbit, HilbertModularForm.heckeField, HilbertModularForm.heckeOrbitDimension_eq_finrank_heckeField")
*Dimension of a Hecke orbit.* ([LMFDB](https://beta.lmfdb.org/knowledge/show/mf.hilbert.dimension))

The *dimension* of a {bpref "mf.hilbert.hecke_orbit"}[Hecke orbit] is its $`\Q`-vector space dimension.  The dimension of the Hecke orbit of a form $`f` is also equal to the degree over $`\Q` of the field generated by the Hecke eigenvalues of $`f`.
:::

:::definition "mf.hilbert.eigenvalue" (lean := "HilbertModularForm.heckeIdealEigenvalueNormalised, HilbertModularForm.IsNormalised, HilbertModularForm.heckeIdealEigenvalue, HilbertModularForm.uEigenvalueNormalised, HilbertModularForm.HasShimuraHeckeEigenvalue")
*Hecke eigenvalues.* ([LMFDB](https://beta.lmfdb.org/knowledge/show/mf.hilbert.eigenvalue))

Let $`f` be an eigenform normalized so that $`a_{(1)} = 1`. The Hecke operator $`T_{\frak{n}}` acts by $`T_{\frak{n}}f = a_{\frak{n}}f` where $`a_{\frak{n}}` is the Hecke eigenvalue.
:::

:::definition "mf.hilbert.hecke_orbit" (lean := "HilbertModularForm.heckeIdealOrbit, HilbertModularForm.heckeIdealOrbit_eq_span, HilbertModularForm.heckeIdealSpace, HilbertModularForm.space, HilbertModularGroup.finite_heckeCosets")
*Hecke orbit for Hilbert modular form.* ([LMFDB](https://beta.lmfdb.org/knowledge/show/mf.hilbert.hecke_orbit))

The Hecke orbit of a {bpref "mf.hilbert"}[Hilbert modular form] $`f` is the subspace of Hilbert modular forms generated by the images of $`f` under the Hecke operators coprime to the level of $`f`.

Depends on: {uses "mf.hilbert"}[]
:::

:::definition "mf.hilbert.label"
*Label of an Hilbert Modular Form.* ([LMFDB](https://beta.lmfdb.org/knowledge/show/mf.hilbert.label))

The label of a Galois orbit of {bpref "mf.hilbert"}[Hilbert cuspforms ] is given by

{bpref "nf.label"}[(number field label)] - (level label) - (orbit label)

separated by dashes.

The level ideal for Hilbert modular forms differs from the {bpref "nf.ideal_labels"}[ideal label] used in other parts of the LMFDB, since this data precedes the invention of these labels.  Right now, the ideal is just labelled N.i where $`N` is the norm and i is a positive integer distinguishing ideals of the same norm.  The ideal $`\mathfrak{N}` is described on the pages by $`[N, n, \beta]` where $`N=\mathrm{Nm}(\mathfrak{N})` is the absolute norm, $`n` generates the ideal $`\mathfrak{N} \cap \Z`, and $`\beta` is some element such that $`\mathfrak{N} = (N, n, \beta)`.

The orbit label is a letter again used to distinguish forms with the same number field and level.

Depends on: {uses "mf.hilbert"}[] {uses "nf.ideal_labels"}[] {uses "nf.label"}[]
:::

:::definition "mf.hilbert.level_norm" (lean := "HilbertModularForm.level, HilbertModularForm.levelNorm")
*Level of a Hilbert modular form.* ([LMFDB](https://beta.lmfdb.org/knowledge/show/mf.hilbert.level_norm))

Given a Hilbert modular form $`f` that is defined in terms of the {bpref "nf.totally_real"}[totally real] {bpref "nf"}[number field] $`F`, the *level* of $`f` is a nonzero ideal $`\mathfrak{N}` of the {bpref "nf.ring_of_integers"}[ring of integers] $`\Z_F` of $`F` that determines the group $`\Gamma_0(\mathfrak{N})` under which $`f` transforms. In the LMFDB, such an ideal $`\mathfrak{N}` is identified by a {bpref "nf.ideal.label.hmf"}[label].

The *level norm* is the absolute norm of the level of the form.

Depends on: {uses "nf"}[] {uses "nf.ring_of_integers"}[] {uses "nf.totally_real"}[]
:::

:::definition "mf.hilbert.q_expansion" (lean := "HilbertModularForm.HasHilbertFourierExpansion, HilbertModularForm.HasHilbertFourierExpansion.unique, HilbertModularForm.fourierCoeff, HilbertModularForm.idealCoeff, HilbertModularForm.idealCoeff_eq")
*q-expansion of a Hilbert modular form.* ([LMFDB](https://beta.lmfdb.org/knowledge/show/mf.hilbert.q_expansion))

Let $`f` be a Hilbert modular form over a totally real field $`F`. Then $`f` admits $`q`-expansions at any cusp of the Hilbert modular group. These cusps are indexed by the strict class group of $`F`.

Given an ideal $`\mathfrak{a}` representing a cusp, the $`q`-expansion of $`f` around this cusp can be described by an infinite series. Translating the cusp to infinity, the form $`f` transforms to a form $`f_{\mathfrak{a}}`. The $`q`-expansion of $`f` at the cusp corresponding to $`\mathfrak{a}` is that of $`f_{\mathfrak{a}}` at infinity. It is represented by an infinite series

$$`f_{\mathfrak{a}}(z) = \sum_{u \in (\mathfrak{ad})_+^{-1}} a_{u\mathfrak{ad}} q^{\mathrm{Tr}(uz)},`

where $`\mathfrak{d}` is the different of the ring of integers of $`F` and where $`q=e^{2\pi i}`. Given an integral ideal $`\frak{n}`, we define the $`\frak{n}^{th}`-fourier coefficient as   $`a_{\frak{n}} \colonequals a_u` where $`u` is a totally positive element satisfying $`(u) = \frak{n}(\frak{ad})^{-1}`. If $`f` is an eigenform, then $`a_{\frak{n}}` is the eigenvalue corresponding to the hecke operator $`T_{\frak{n}}`.
:::

:::definition "mf.hilbert.weight_vector" (lean := "HilbertModularForm.weight, HilbertModularForm.IsParallelWeight")
*Weight of a Hilbert modular form.* ([LMFDB](https://beta.lmfdb.org/knowledge/show/mf.hilbert.weight_vector))

The *weight* of a {bpref "mf.hilbert"}[Hilbert modular form] $`f` is the vector of positive integers $`(k_1\ldots,k_n)` occurring in the transformation formula for $`f`. When the $`k_i` are all equal to $`k \in \Z_{>0}`, we say $`f` has *parallel weight* $`k`.

Depends on: {uses "mf.hilbert"}[]
:::
